WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.320
INTRO:
Ford.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:16.320
The company that pioneered the modern car,
the assembly line, the 40 hour work week,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.880
and the creator of America’s best selling
vehicle: the F150.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:27.369
Despite this impressive and long lasting legacy,
Ford is currently in a rather dire situation.

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:33.280
They’ve got over $200 billion worth of liabilities
which generally puts them in the top 10 list

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.530
of most indebted companies.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.490
While Ford doesn’t usually come in at the
number one spot on these lists, something

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:42.920
that we should note is that all of the other
companies on this list generally have a market

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.430
cap thats several times larger than Ford.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.890
So, while Ford may not be the most indebted
company in the world, they’re usually the

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.420
most leveraged company in the world.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.250
Thankfully, they do have more assets than
liabilities given that their assets come in

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.440
at $257 billion.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.220
But, when we compare their cash reserves to
their liabilities, it doesn’t look nearly

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.220
as pretty.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.030
Ford currently only has $20 billion worth
cash and cash equivalents.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:14.780
In other words, their total liabilites is
10 times their current cash reserves.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.450
Considering this, I don’t think you’d
be surprised to hear that Macroaxis gives

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.409
the company a 44% chance of bankruptcy.

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:27.229
Now, it should be noted that Ford is the only
American legacy automaker that has never filed

00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:29.000
for bankruptcy in the past.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.860
But, this may actually come back to haunt
them because they never got a reset switch

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.009
like the other automakers.

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:40.900
And this is one of the main reasons they have
insane debt levels in the first place.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.790
So, is it just a matter of time until Ford
goes bankrupt?

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:51.930
ONE BAD LEADER:
Taking a look back, Ford’s liquidity problems

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:56.280
date way further than just the pandemic or
even the 2008 financial recession.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.000
In fact, the root of Ford’s financial problems
can be traced back to the late 1990s when

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.710
a man named Jacques Nasser was appointed as
the new CEO.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.380
Jacques was an entrepreneur at heart.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.732
In fact, he had been running businesses since
he was a teenager.

00:02:10.732 --> 00:02:14.950
He had a small bicycle-making operation and
he ran multiple disco clubs.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:18.870
So, it was only natural that he brought his
entrepreneurial spirit into Ford.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.590
Jacques didn’t just come in as an executive
though.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:26.340
He actually joined as modest financial analyst
at age 20 in 1968.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:32.510
And it wasn’t till 31 years later in 1999
that Jacques would become CEO.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.550
In the meantime, he had made a name for himself
as a shrewd businessman.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.290
Many of his colleagues actually nicknamed
him Jac the Knife and Black Jack.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.630
Jac the Knife was a reference to how many
jobs he had cut over the years.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:47.480
And Black Jack was a reference to his intoleration
for unprofitable sectors.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:53.040
While these are by no means the kindest of
traits, many successful CEOs share these traits,

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.260
so this in itself didn’t taint Jacques’
tenure.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.320
What tainted his tenure and screwed over Ford
financially was his business decisions themselves.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.670
First of all, Jacques tried to transform Ford
in some sort of lifestyle company.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.370
He wanted people to have a high opinion of
Ford, so he started buying out luxury competitors.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:17.140
Two of these acquisitions included Land Rover
for $3.3 billion and Volvo for $6.47 billion.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.070
Not only were these expensive purchases, but
it also led to Ford having to split their

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:25.040
attention between the companies which led
to all of the brands suffering.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.570
Aside from trying to make Ford into a lifestyle
company, Jacques also placed a massive emphasis

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:30.850
on the internet.

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:35.040
Being an entrepreneur at heart, it was obvious
to Jacques that the internet was the next

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.260
big thing.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.880
And he strongly felt that the internet should
play a significant role at Ford.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.700
So, he spent loads of money developing Ford’s
website, affiliate marketing, dealer portals,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.040
and so on.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:50.660
These efforts were actually pretty successful
given that Ford garnered millions of visitors

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.199
every single month.

00:03:52.199 --> 00:03:56.690
Looking back, these were pretty visionary
moves, but Jacques missed the mark in two

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:57.690
areas.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.470
First of all, he was diverting so much attention
into the internet that it was taking away

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:05.230
from Ford’s core business which was making
good cars.

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:09.600
While having a good online presence is definitely
important, as a car company, you’re number

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.950
1 focus should be making the best cars you
can, and that was unfortunately no longer

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.449
the case with Ford.

00:04:15.449 --> 00:04:19.320
As for Jacques’ second short fall, his timing
was terrible.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.639
Not only was Jacques too early for his time,
but he was doing all of this right before

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:24.979
the dotcom bubble burst.

00:04:24.979 --> 00:04:30.130
So, when internet companies crashed 90 to
95% in the early 2000s, Jacques looked like

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.580
an absolute idiot.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.770
And to a certain degree, he was.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:38.010
He had completely dismantled Ford’s focus
on creating cars while also burning billions

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.310
on acquisitions and the internet.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.790
Hearing all this, I don’t think you’d
be surprised to hear that Ford would replace

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:48.460
Jacques in late 2001, but the damage had already
been done.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:55.150
NARROWLY AVOIDING BANKRUPTCY:
While Jacques was out of the company, Ford’s

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.050
pain had just begun.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:01.500
First of all, the acquisitions that Jacques
had completed weren’t performing that well,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.210
they were actually performing terribly.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:08.460
But, wait a minute, Land Rover and Volvo were
only a couple billion dollars each.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.430
So, how much could this possibly hurt Ford?

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:15.850
Well, while the companies themselves weren’t
all that expensive for Ford’s standards,

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:19.470
the same could not be said about their balance
sheets and cash flow.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:23.610
You see, when you acquire a company, you don’t
just acquire the good parts like the brand

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:25.400
image and the customers.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.290
You also acquire the bad parts like their
debt and negative cash flow.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:35.070
It’s estimated that holding Jaguar and Land
Rover cost Ford $35 to $50 billion.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:38.610
A similar story could be seen with Volvo as
well.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.390
Not only were these companies a massive drag
on Ford’s income statements while they owned

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:46.480
them, but Ford would also lose a lot of money
trying to offload these companies.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:52.180
For example, Ford would end up selling Volvo
for just $1.5 billion.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.480
As a reminder, they paid $6.47 billion to
acquire the company.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:02.050
Similarly, they would end up selling Jaguar
and Land Rover for $2.3 billion.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:07.010
Just 9 years before this, Ford paid $3.3 billion
for Land Rover alone.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:12.960
Clearly, these were massive losses for Ford,
but this was just the beginning of their problems.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.630
You see, oil prices would reach nearly $200
per barrel in mid 2008.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:23.120
For perspective, if you thought oil prices
were high a couple of months ago, these prices

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.840
were 60% higher than that.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.820
So, I don’t think you’d be surprised to
hear that people shied away from buying large

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:33.400
pick up trucks and SUVs given that they would
lose a fortune just paying for gas.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:37.990
And given that large pick up trucks and SUVs
were Ford’s speciality, they were basically

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:38.990
screwed.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:42.880
To make things even worse, the US was facing
a serious recession in 2008 unlike the small

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:43.880
technical recession that we’re in right
now.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.550
This simply led to Ford’s sales declining
even more.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:52.190
All of this would climax in Ford losing a
record $14.6 billion in 2008.

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:56.810
And at this point, it was only a matter of
time until the government stepped in and bailed

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:58.030
out the company.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:02.780
Ford would end up receiving a $5.9 billion
loan from the Department of Energy which allowed

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:07.450
them to narrowly avoid bankruptcy and start
focusing on developing energy efficient vehicles.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:11.480
But, unfortunately for Ford, this was terrible
timing as well.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.990
You see, oil prices would crash hard over
the next couple of years which meant that

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:20.140
Americans could once again indulge in their
love for trucks and SUVs.

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:24.260
Given that Ford was preoccupied with cars
like the Ford Focus, this gave GM the perfect

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:28.610
opportunity to make strides with their Tahoe,
Suburban, and Escalade series.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:33.150
And before you knew it, Ford was falling behind
in the same industry that they had dominated

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.150
for decades.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:39.461
So, while Ford was the only legacy American
automaker to avoid bankruptcy, it’s not

00:07:39.461 --> 00:07:44.750
like things turned out any better for them
anway.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:49.020
CROSSROADS:
Over the past several years, Ford has shifted

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:52.340
their focus back onto what they’re good
at: trucks and SUVs.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.100
They’ve even gone ahead and announced that
they’re gonna completely retire the Ford

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.350
Focus in 2025.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.940
It seems that this approach has worked out
pretty well for Ford given that they’ve

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:05.610
been able to make the F-150 the number 1 selling
vehicle once again.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.570
But, it’s not all good news.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:12.570
Ford has never been able to work out of the
debt mess that they got into in the 2000s.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:17.530
Sometimes, their liabilities stand at $150
billion and sometimes their liabilities stand

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:19.060
at $250 billion.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:23.410
But, for the most part, Ford hasn’t been
able to reduce their average liabilities to

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.270
under $200 billion.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:29.430
To make things worse, the automotive industry
has been facing a pretty big cross road over

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.699
the past couple of years with EVs.

00:08:31.699 --> 00:08:35.919
Fortunately, Ford has not been ignoring EVs
like Toyota.

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:40.880
Ford has actually been taking EVs extremely
seriously, and they were the first legacy

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.340
automaker to convert a popular gas model to
fully electric.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:48.040
So, they’re definitely on the right track,
but this doesn’t mean that the path forward

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:49.589
will be easy.

00:08:49.589 --> 00:08:53.670
Profitability scaling EV production is extremely
challenging and Ford is experiencing this

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.350
first hand as we speak.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.520
Take the Mach E for example.

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:02.170
Ford started Mach E production in Q3 of 2020,
and they were able to ramp production for

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:03.170
3 quarters.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:07.660
But, eversince then, they’ve not only been
stagnating but declining.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:11.200
When you put the Mach E’s production ramp
next to the Model 3’s production ramp, you

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.070
can really start to see how far behind Ford
is.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:19.180
By the 7th quarter of production, Tesla was
pumping out over 70,000 model 3s while Ford

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:20.840
was struggling to pump out 15,000.

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:25.870
Now, it’s not like this production ramp
was easy for Tesla given that they nearly

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.800
went bankrupt trying to reach their production
targets.

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:33.460
What this means for Ford is that they have
yet to cross the hardest stage which is scaling.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:37.710
To make things worse, Ford is having to do
all this during a historic supply shortage.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.870
According to Ford’s CFO, inflation along
with the supply shortage have completely wiped

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:43.640
out the Mach E’s profit margin.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:47.950
So, it’s just gonna be even harder for Ford
to reach scale than it was for Tesla.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:53.270
Not to mention, Tesla never had to deal with
$200 billion worth of liabilities either.

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:58.080
So, at this point, it seems like all the cards
are stacked against Ford whether its their

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.380
balance sheet or their production capabilities,
but one attribute that is working in their

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:04.200
favor is demand.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:09.580
Ford has over 200,000 Lightning reservations,
and given the popularity of the F150, that

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:12.190
number will likely only go up with time.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:16.649
So, Ford definitely has the demand to scale
their EV business to the next level and remain

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:18.390
a popular automaker.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:21.200
The question, however, is: will the company
survive the transition.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:29.420
THE STATE OF FORD:
So far, I know this video has been rather

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:34.470
negative on Ford, but they deserve a lot of
credit for many of their choices.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.950
At no point has Ford tried to half ass the
transition to EVs.

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:44.040
The company is literally all in on EVs having
committed to invest $50 billion into EVs by

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:45.040
2026.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:49.040
Also, there doesn’t seem to be any doubts
amongst the leadership either.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:53.160
It seems like they all unanimously believe
that successfully transitioning to EVs is

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:55.120
the only way forward.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:59.790
This sort of confidence and clarity is exactly
what every automaker needs.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:04.779
And honestly, in my opinion, if any legacy
automaker is gonna make it to the other side,

00:11:04.779 --> 00:11:06.330
it’s gonna be Ford.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:11.980
But, with all of that being said, it’s not
gonna be an easy journey by any means.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:16.220
Jacques well intentioned but poorly executed
initiatives from 20 years ago are still weighing

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:17.690
down the company to this day.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:21.560
And honestly, there’s no easy way out for
Ford.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:25.280
All they can do is continue doing what they’re
already doing.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:28.770
Try their best to transition to EVs while
also protecting the financial integrity of

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:31.100
the company as much as possible.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:35.740
But only time will tell if their ambitious
plans actually end up working out.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:37.930
Do you think Ford will make it to the other
side?

